  "Number": 11,
  "Question": "The number of roots satisfying the equation \\(\\sqrt{5-x}=x\\sqrt{5-x}\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "unlimited",
    "B": "\\(3\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\)",
    "D": "\\(1\\)",
    "E": "\\(0\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The same square root appears on both sides, but it may be zero, so we must separate that case before dividing. The square root is real exactly when \\(5-x\\ge0\\), or \\(x\\le5\\).\n\nIf \\(x=5\\), both sides are zero, so \\(5\\) is a solution. If \\(x<5\\), then \\(\\sqrt{5-x}>0\\), and dividing both sides by it gives\n\\[\n1=x.\n\\]\nThe value \\(x=1\\) satisfies the original equation because both sides equal \\(2\\). These cases exhaust the domain, giving exactly \\(\\boxed{2}\\) roots, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
